import{_ as e,c as t,o as n,a4 as a}from"./chunks/framework.P4k6vdAm.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Key Concepts","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/overview/key-concepts.md","filePath":"docs/overview/key-concepts.md","lastUpdated":1793099931000}'),o={name:"docs/overview/key-concepts.md"},s=a('<h1 id="key-concepts" tabindex="-1">Key Concepts <a class="header-anchor" href="#key-concepts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Key Concepts&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page introduces the ideas the rest of the documentation builds on. Each section links to the detailed page.</p><h2 id="tenants" tabindex="-1">Tenants <a class="header-anchor" href="#tenants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tenants&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Everything you create in AireGlu belongs to a <strong>tenant</strong>: endpoints, jobs, secrets, variables, connections and so on. A tenant is isolated from other tenants, and the tenant name forms part of every endpoint URL. What each user can see and do within a tenant is controlled by permissions in <a href="https://docs.identity.aireinnovate.com/docs/getting-started/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">AireIdentity</a>.</p><h2 id="endpoints" tabindex="-1">Endpoints <a class="header-anchor" href="#endpoints" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Endpoints&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An <strong>endpoint</strong> is the core building block. It&#39;s an HTTP API, hosted by AireGlu, that runs a configured series of steps whenever it&#39;s called. Its URL is made from your tenant and the endpoint&#39;s name:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>https://glu-api.aireinnovate.com/{tenant}/{endpoint-name}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Endpoints are <strong>versioned</strong>. Each time you save changes you can create a new version, and one version is marked as the <strong>default</strong>. Calls to the plain URL above run the default version, and you can add a version to the URL to call a specific one. This lets you test a new version before switching callers over. See <a href="/docs/triggering/running.html">Running an Endpoint</a>.</p><h2 id="how-an-endpoint-runs" tabindex="-1">How an endpoint runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-an-endpoint-runs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How an endpoint runs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every endpoint run goes through the same pipeline, and the endpoint editor shows it as nodes in this order:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span> Caller ──▶ Authentication ──▶ Input ──▶ Task 1 ──▶ Task 2 ──▶ ... ──▶ Response</span></span></code></pre></div><ol><li><strong><a href="/docs/endpoint-authentication/">Authentication</a></strong> - checks the caller is allowed to run the endpoint (API key, IP range, OAuth bearer token and so on). If several methods are configured, all of them must pass.</li><li><strong><a href="/docs/endpoint-input/input.html">Input</a></strong> - validates the request body against the endpoint&#39;s schema. Invalid input is rejected with a <code>400</code> before any task runs.</li><li><strong><a href="/docs/tasks/">Tasks</a></strong> - run in order. Each task does one thing: call an API, map data, send an email, and so on. Each task can use the input and the results of the tasks before it.</li><li><strong>Response</strong> - one of the task results becomes the HTTP response. Which one depends on the tasks used and the <code>Stop on Task Failure</code> setting - see <a href="/docs/building/endpoint-response.html">How the Endpoint Response is Decided</a>.</li></ol><h2 id="working-with-data" tabindex="-1">Working with data <a class="header-anchor" href="#working-with-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Working with data&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Task settings are rarely fixed values. They&#39;re built from data that&#39;s only known when the endpoint runs:</p><ul><li><strong>Paths</strong> insert a value from the input or from an earlier task&#39;s result, e.g. <code>--*email*--</code>.</li><li><strong><a href="/docs/building/liquid.html">Liquid templates</a></strong> handle anything more complex, such as loops, conditions and reshaping, e.g. <span><code>{{ input.email }}</code></span>.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong> provide built-in utilities, e.g. <code>--*AG:UUID()*--</code> or <code>--*AG:DateTime(dd-MM-yyyy)*--</code>.</li><li><strong>Filters</strong> decide whether a task runs at all.</li></ul><p>See <a href="/docs/building/working-with-data.html">Working with Data</a>.</p><h2 id="secrets-and-variables" tabindex="-1">Secrets and variables <a class="header-anchor" href="#secrets-and-variables" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Secrets and variables&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rather than hardcoding values into endpoints, store them once per tenant and reference them from any task:</p><ul><li><strong><a href="/docs/connections/secrets.html">Secrets</a></strong> are for sensitive values such as passwords and API keys, and can also hold files. They can never be viewed after they&#39;re saved, not even in logs or debug data.</li><li><strong><a href="/docs/connections/variables.html">Variables</a></strong> are for non-sensitive settings such as base URLs or email addresses. They can be <strong>scoped</strong> to give a different value to a particular endpoint or endpoint version.</li></ul><h2 id="inbound-authentication-vs-outbound-connections" tabindex="-1">Inbound authentication vs outbound connections <a class="header-anchor" href="#inbound-authentication-vs-outbound-connections" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Inbound authentication vs outbound connections&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These are easy to confuse because some of them use the same mechanisms, such as OAuth client credentials:</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><a href="/docs/endpoint-authentication/">Endpoint Authentication</a></th><th><a href="/docs/connections/">Connections</a></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direction</td><td><strong>Inbound</strong> - who can call <em>your</em> endpoint</td><td><strong>Outbound</strong> - how your endpoint calls <em>other</em> systems</td></tr><tr><td>Configured on</td><td>Each endpoint&#39;s Authentication node</td><td>Once per tenant, then selected in tasks</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Only accept calls with a valid AireIdentity bearer token</td><td>Get an OAuth token to call a third-party API, or send email through Office 365</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="ways-to-trigger-an-endpoint" tabindex="-1">Ways to trigger an endpoint <a class="header-anchor" href="#ways-to-trigger-an-endpoint" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ways to trigger an endpoint&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An endpoint doesn&#39;t have to be called directly over HTTP. It can also be run by:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Trigger</th><th>When it runs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="/docs/triggering/running.html">Direct HTTP call</a></td><td>When a system or user calls the endpoint URL</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/docs/triggering/route-mapping.html">Route Mapping</a></td><td>When a call is made to a custom URL that you&#39;ve mapped to the endpoint</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/docs/triggering/jobs.html">Job</a></td><td>On a schedule, like a cron job</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/docs/triggering/receivers.html">Receiver</a></td><td>When an email, GovNotify SMS or MESH message arrives</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/docs/triggering/batch-invocation.html">Batch invocation</a></td><td>Once per item in a batch, asynchronously</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/docs/tasks/aireglu-endpoint-request.html">Another endpoint</a></td><td>From an AireGlu Endpoint Request task</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="tracing-and-debugging" tabindex="-1">Tracing and debugging <a class="header-anchor" href="#tracing-and-debugging" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tracing and debugging&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every run gets a <strong>transaction ID</strong>. It&#39;s shared with any endpoints, jobs or batch items that the run triggers, so a whole chain of calls can be viewed together in <a href="/docs/operations/metrics.html">Metrics › Usage Data</a>. Turn on <a href="/docs/operations/debug-mode.html">Debug Mode</a> for an endpoint version to also record each task&#39;s inputs and outputs.</p><h2 id="managing-endpoints-as-code" tabindex="-1">Managing endpoints as code <a class="header-anchor" href="#managing-endpoints-as-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Managing endpoints as code&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Endpoint definitions can be exported, imported, and kept in your own Git <strong><a href="/docs/operations/repositories.html">Repository</a></strong>, which AireGlu can sync from automatically. This lets you handle your own backups and keep endpoints under version control.</p><h2 id="ai-tools" tabindex="-1">AI tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-tools" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI tools&quot;">​</a></h2><p>AireGlu can expose endpoints to AI assistants as tools and resources through <strong><a href="/docs/mcp-servers/">MCP Servers</a></strong>. It can also host <strong><a href="/docs/agents/">Agents</a></strong> (preview) that use those tools. Both are enabled per tenant.</p>',31),i=[s];function r(d,h,c,l,p,u){return n(),t("div",null,i)}const b=e(o,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,b as default};
